cture (Last-In, First-Out) using a Python class and tests push, pop, peek, size, and is_empty.\",\n34:     code: `class Stack:\n35:     def __init__(self):\n36:         self.items = []\n37: \n38:     def push(self, item):\n39:         self.items.append(item)\n40: \n41:     def pop(self):\n42:         if not self.is_empty():\n43:             return self.items.pop()\n44:         return None\n45: \n46:     def peek(self):\n47:         if not self.is_empty():\n48:             return self.items[-1]\n49:         return None\n50: \n51:     def is_empty(self):\n52:         return len(self.items) == 0\n53: \n54:     def size(self):\n55:         return len(self.items)\n56: \n57: \n58: # Example usage\n59: s = Stack()\n60: s.push(10)\nThe above content does NOT show the entire file contents. If you need to view any lines of the file which were not shown to complete your task, call this tool again to view those lines.\n"}
